<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gelug</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1409</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Gelug Buddhism, Yellow Hat school</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gelug is the school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> founded by the reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsongkhapa">Tsongkhapa</a> (1357–1419), stressing monastic discipline and scholastic study. Its leading lamas gained Mongol patrons when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altan-khan">Altan Khan</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonam-gyatso">Sonam Gyatso</a> the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a> in 1578, and in the following decades the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> adopted Gelug <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. With Khoshut Mongol help it became the ruling school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> in 1642.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gelug.jpg" alt="Gelug" width="320" height="521" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tsonkapa,_16th_century,_Collection_of_Rubin_Museum_of_Art.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
